WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.071
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:12.224
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Well Mayor Immanuel

00:00:12.224 --> 00:00:14.810
it's a great honor to
be here with you today.

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:18.250
I know you've been traveling the country
promoting Chicago as a center for

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:19.570
entrepreneurship, and

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:23.780
many of the 400-plus GSB students here
are interested in being entrepreneurs.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.945
So, why should they go to Chicago
rather than Silicon Valley?

00:00:26.945 --> 00:00:29.810
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:34.060
first of all,
since we're out here, Chicago,

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:38.420
there was a comparison of ten of
the biggest cities in the United States.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:41.418
It has the lowest cost of living
of any of the major 10 cities.

00:00:41.418 --> 00:00:47.220
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.620
&gt;&gt; You can actually get an apartment with

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:50.075
a view and still afford a meal.

00:00:50.075 --> 00:00:53.630
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm saying that out here.

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:56.730
The second thing is I'll say this.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:57.729
This is serious thing.

00:00:57.729 --> 00:01:00.000
Time Out Magazine did
a thing of worldwide cities.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.760
And was rated as the best,
number one city to live in in the world.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.320
And my kids tease me often,
because I'm a big booster of Chicago.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:07.310
But I've got to be honest.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:10.015
I was a little shocked at that evaluation,
myself.

00:01:10.015 --> 00:01:12.230
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But on the entrepreneurship.

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:15.890
Not to name another business school,
so I apologize.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:19.310
I'll have to go into a witness
protection plan now.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:22.520
Harvard Business did a case study of
Chicago's entrepreneurial culture and

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:23.160
high tech.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.550
It's going to come out in a couple months.

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:30.580
And it talks about what the unlike
what happened here in San Francisco

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:34.330
in the valley, we've done it from
a more diversified basis, and

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.740
what's created now in the city of Chicago.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:41.120
And the Harvard Business study says it's
a classic study of what public sector,

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.160
private sector has happened.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.490
And in looking across the students, I
should also say there was a study came out

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:49.975
that Chicago was the number one city for
female entrepreneurs.

00:01:49.975 --> 00:01:55.580
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And we a lot of emphasis in that space.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:00.990
And I think it's Pitch Magazine rated
Chicago as the number one city for

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:02.099
return on VC money.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.685
So, now those are all my facts and
I'm done now.

00:02:05.685 --> 00:02:06.910
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:10.975
the other thing is it's raining here,
and it's about 15 degrees in Chicago.

00:02:10.975 --> 00:02:12.370
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:16.520
we actually can use our lakefront, but
there's a great quality of life to it.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:17.480
And I also think,

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.870
I always used to refer to Chicago as
the most American of American cities.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:25.420
You know we have 140 languages
spoken in our public schools.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.880
And I was at a school the other day
where we were doing an addition and

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:32.080
26 different languages came up to
say welcome to the city where we're

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.080
making a critical capital investment.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:35.980
And that's America,

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:40.300
that's the city of Chicago and I think
that's the strength is our diversity.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:45.490
And whether it's b to b, b to c, you can
create a company, you can start a company.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:49.380
And you have a very supportive community.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:51.410
We have a community culture
that's true of our theater.

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:52.580
That's true of our business community.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.660
And it's true of our
entrepreneurial community,

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:59.070
which is why you see people
succeed even when they fail.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.620
Which is your greatest
learning experience.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:05.390
Have the support to continue to go on and
push on, which is true both in theater,

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:09.190
entrepreneurship and,
obviously, in politics as well.

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:10.150
&gt;&gt; You mentioned education.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:11.970
It was a passion of yours.

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:13.600
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; What are you doing in Chicago

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.781
to help those who want to be educated?

00:03:16.781 --> 00:03:21.025
&gt;&gt; Well, I hope it doesn't turn on
that last point, want to be educated.

00:03:21.025 --> 00:03:23.960
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, we did a number of things, and

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:24.910
I want to be honest.

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:25.982
I ran for mayor.

00:03:25.982 --> 00:03:29.980
One, it's my home,

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:34.090
it's a city that a hundred years ago
this year my grandfather came to,

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:38.510
13 years old by himself,
leaving the pogroms of Eastern Europe.

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:40.420
And his grandson is
the mayor of that city.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:43.175
It's only in America, and
I'll tell you, only in Chicago.

00:03:43.175 --> 00:03:46.055
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And

00:03:46.055 --> 00:03:47.190
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:48.480
&gt;&gt; My grandfather was a dreamer.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.190
13 years old, by himself, advantage
to meet a third cousin he never knew.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:04:00.430
And through meat cutting, steel working,
truck driving his grandson's,

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:03.925
one is a Hollywood, I don't know
what you would describe Ari as.

00:04:03.925 --> 00:04:06.560
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The other one is a doctor and

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:10.740
his other grandson,
is an elect in public service.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:14.170
That's in a great American story and
that's the strength of this country.

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:17.340
And he just basically followed
his dreams and allowed us.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:22.510
So I believe and I ran for mayor, not just
because I wanted to pay tribute to my

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:26.280
grandfather but I believe firming
what the city has to offer.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.570
So we've done a couple of things.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:31.790
The first thing I made kindergarten
full day kindergarten.

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:33.590
Universal for every child.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:36.970
It use to be half the kids had seven
hours a day, half had four and

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:39.060
trust me the kids that are getting
four should have gotten seven.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:43.180
The kids that were getting seven
could have afforded to get four.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:45.870
It was, I suppose I could say you could
get me out I was only at 51 minutes.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:49.180
It was ass backwards, to be blunt.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:51.210
So we made universal kindergarten.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:55.340
We added an hour and 15 minute to
every day and two weeks to every year.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:56.710
So every child now gets two and

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:59.850
a half more years of school
education than they did before.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:04.136
The largest increase ever in
the history of the United States.

00:05:04.136 --> 00:05:06.735
Now, we also did a couple of other things.

00:05:06.735 --> 00:05:10.600
One, I think the debate about education
is too focused on teachers and

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.420
not enough about parents and principals.

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:16.430
No teacher can afford to
do it all by themselves.

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:18.780
A school principal runs that building so

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:22.730
we reinforced them to be held
accountable for results.

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:26.100
Finding teachers and
having a recruitment system on that.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.580
We've reconverted our high schools
to be college preparatory.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:34.400
End result, on the NAEP test last year our
eighth graders led the United States in

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:35.890
math gains.

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:39.770
Our fourth graders were third in
reading gains of urban systems.

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:42.650
There were three school districts in
the United States whose fourth and

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:44.610
eighth grader's reading went up.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:48.570
Miami, Washington DC,
and the City of Chicago.

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.710
When I became mayor,
our graduation was 57%.

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:54.630
Our freshmen on track right now is 87%.

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:58.770
We match the United States, kids going
on to a four year institution and

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:00.410
kids going on to community college.

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:01.800
And trust me,

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:05.070
the demographic of the city Chicago
is not the same as the United States.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:07.910
And where the first city in
the United States that if you going to be

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:11.498
average in high school
community college is free.

00:06:11.498 --> 00:06:15.061
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And

00:06:15.061 --> 00:06:20.304
if it's the only scholarship
public refunded where undocumented

00:06:20.304 --> 00:06:24.645
kids can go to college,
only one in the United States.

00:06:24.645 --> 00:06:29.170
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And, which we're also excited about is,

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:33.530
if you get a B average in high school,
community college is free.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:37.770
And it's the second largest system in
the United States that the World Bank

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:41.130
called the best college career
program in the United States.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:43.430
If you keep the B average
in community college,

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.410
all the schools in the City of Chicago,
16 of them.

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:49.050
You got to get the right ACT but

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:53.210
if you keep the b average, they give you
a 40% discount on tuition, on average.

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:56.840
It's the only place in America you can
go to college for basically a year and

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:58.370
a half at cost.

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:01.140
And that is the single greatest
pressure point on parents.

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:04.660
Now, I'll say this as I've said
to my kids and I'll say here.

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:06.260
I don't know all of you obviously.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:08.100
I just walked on.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:13.020
We've got two things in common, the love
of our parents and a good education.

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:14.660
I can't do anything about number one,

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:17.160
but I can do something in
public policy about number two.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:22.880
And I have to guarantee parents
a quality education for

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:24.850
their children, they pick what's right.

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:27.910
So we put principles first,
school principles.

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:32.350
Reinvented high school so it's relevant
to college, community college for career.

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:35.418
Made kindergarten universal full day,

00:07:35.418 --> 00:07:40.520
pre-k where 60% of our kids
now get a full day pre-K.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:45.220
And we've been willing to take on failure,
whether that's consolidation of schools,

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:47.190
turning around schools.

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:51.030
And I also don't see myself as a reformer.

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:52.230
I think that's nuts.

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:57.160
Nobody sits around the kitchen
table going hey let's go to reform.

00:07:58.690 --> 00:07:59.630
Trust me.

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:03.530
You sit around and
go Either quality or mediocrity.

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:05.333
And I want quality for my kids.

00:08:05.333 --> 00:08:09.260
And the goal should not be reform,
the goal should be quality.

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:14.205
And then what fits into
quality versus mediocrity.

00:08:14.205 --> 00:08:20.746
So we now have the largest international
baccalaureate system in the United States.

00:08:20.746 --> 00:08:23.260
We have every branch
of the armed services.

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:24.840
Runs a high school in
the city of Chicago and

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.350
the largest Junior ROTC
in the United States.

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:32.699
And I say that here just so you all know,
every kid competing to get in on it,

00:08:32.699 --> 00:08:36.928
there's eight applicants for every high
school seat that's run by military.

00:08:36.928 --> 00:08:40.655
And 80% of those kids graduate,
and 90% on to college.

00:08:40.655 --> 00:08:42.099
And it's all kids of color.

00:08:42.099 --> 00:08:46.505
Unbelievable system, and
we also have seven of the ten best high

00:08:46.505 --> 00:08:49.778
schools in the state are in
the city of Chicago.

00:08:49.778 --> 00:08:52.553
And so that's our story and
we're sticking to it.

00:08:52.553 --> 00:08:55.517
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; With change comes challenge especially

00:08:55.517 --> 00:08:58.686
as a political leader and you're
balancing a lot of competing interests.

00:08:58.686 --> 00:09:01.809
So as the mayor of a large city, how do
you think about negotiating through that

00:09:01.809 --> 00:09:04.669
process to get to the place where you
want to actually affect outcomes?

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:09.371
Well, you know you to weigh equities okay.

00:09:09.371 --> 00:09:16.115
There’s never look,
life is a serious a trade off okay.

00:09:16.115 --> 00:09:21.239
That’s not different in public policy,
but you're going to have to weigh as I do

00:09:21.239 --> 00:09:26.534
when I go through budgets or exercise,
I always say to my staff pain to pleasure.

00:09:26.534 --> 00:09:27.409
What I mean by that,

00:09:27.409 --> 00:09:30.451
I can see by the stunned faces that
was really insightful by the mayor.

00:09:30.451 --> 00:09:34.638
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; How much pain am I ready to give

00:09:34.638 --> 00:09:35.980
to how much gain?

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:40.930
And I'll give you this one story
It was about the third day we

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:43.518
were having a teacher strike.

00:09:43.518 --> 00:09:45.918
It was over the longer school day,
in the longer school day, and

00:09:45.918 --> 00:09:48.100
I made a pledge when I ran,
we were going to do it.

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:52.605
Chicago before I became Mayor
had the shortest school day in

00:09:52.605 --> 00:09:54.955
the United States of America.

00:09:54.955 --> 00:09:58.531
If 85% of your kids are in poverty,
would you design

00:09:58.531 --> 00:10:02.903
as a ticket out of poverty a five
hour day, five and a half hour day?

00:10:02.903 --> 00:10:04.558
That's what we have.

00:10:04.558 --> 00:10:10.060
We had kids out of high school going on to
the streets of Chicago at 2:20, really?

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:15.514
And we couldn't afford recess because
we couldn't give up the academic time,

00:10:15.514 --> 00:10:19.806
which everyone knows kids need recess,
it's actually conducive, the academics,

00:10:19.806 --> 00:10:20.651
that exercise.

00:10:20.651 --> 00:10:23.380
So anyways, about the second or
third day kids are, you know,

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:27.830
teachers are outside my house screaming
at me, screaming at my kids, whatever.

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:32.380
And I'm about to walk out and
Amy looks at me and goes.

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:35.260
I've seen you through an impeachment,
I've seen you through healthcare.

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:38.068
I've seen you through NAFTA, I've seen
you through the assault weapon ban.

00:10:38.068 --> 00:10:41.160
She goes, I've never seen you
calmer at like one of these.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:44.680
I said I have never felt more
right about what I was doing.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:49.263
And then I walked out the front door.

00:10:49.263 --> 00:10:51.633
I just would like the record
to show I kept it clean here.

00:10:51.633 --> 00:10:54.578
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:10:54.578 --> 00:10:59.421
you have to weigh equities constantly,
&gt;&gt; And

00:10:59.421 --> 00:11:02.819
you have to look at what is
a core versus what's not.

00:11:02.819 --> 00:11:04.071
And I'll give you one thing.

00:11:04.071 --> 00:11:07.589
I hope this is helpful from a negotiation.

00:11:07.589 --> 00:11:12.229
Bruce Reed and I were responsible in
the balanced budget agreement in '97 for

00:11:12.229 --> 00:11:13.831
getting kids health care.

00:11:13.831 --> 00:11:17.770
It was kids who where two parents
worked but for Medicaid they were

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:22.150
too high income, yet the private
sector they didn't have healthcare.

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:28.981
And so President Clinton had
proposed pediatric care and

00:11:28.981 --> 00:11:33.813
eye and dental, but
part of Medicaid expansion.

00:11:33.813 --> 00:11:39.900
Gingrich responded, pediatric care, no
eye and dental, and outside of Medicaid.

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:43.254
Well, it's pretty straightforward.

00:11:43.254 --> 00:11:45.461
I mean, now in retrospect, it looks
straightforward, so we said, okay,

00:11:45.461 --> 00:11:46.840
here's the deal.

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.628
We'll do pediatric care,
plus eye and dental,

00:11:49.628 --> 00:11:53.232
which is what President Clinton's
healthcare proposal, but

00:11:53.232 --> 00:11:57.938
we'll do your idea outside of Medicaid,
which was a cigarette tax but separate.

00:11:57.938 --> 00:12:01.490
It was called KidCare,
separate from Medicaid.

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:03.847
They got their win because
they hate entitlements.

00:12:03.847 --> 00:12:07.107
They didn't want to see Medicaid expanded,
and

00:12:07.107 --> 00:12:11.753
President Clinton got his win,
which was expansion of KidCare, or

00:12:11.753 --> 00:12:16.006
creation of KidCare, but
with eye and dental as part of care.

00:12:16.006 --> 00:12:19.793
And so, everybody got something
that was core to their effort, and

00:12:19.793 --> 00:12:23.247
we could have sat there beating
the crap out of each other, but

00:12:23.247 --> 00:12:27.363
at the end of the day Who is finding out
a place which was a good enough win for

00:12:27.363 --> 00:12:30.776
them and a good enough win for
the president to move forward.

00:12:30.776 --> 00:12:33.790
And Bruce Reed and I and
Gene Sperling were the key.

00:12:33.790 --> 00:12:37.577
And you never really want to
give up on your end goals.

00:12:37.577 --> 00:12:42.218
But you gotta be very, very flexible
about your means towards that end, and

00:12:42.218 --> 00:12:46.050
never confuse what your
means are with your ends.

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:50.328
And sometimes, it's very hard in
the midst of the battle where if you're

00:12:50.328 --> 00:12:52.891
middle child and
you want to win every point.

00:12:52.891 --> 00:12:55.164
A moment of self reflection,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:12:55.164 --> 00:12:58.030
&gt;&gt; That don't, remember what your goal is.

00:12:59.420 --> 00:13:04.585
And then be strong about that,
but flexible on the road

00:13:04.585 --> 00:13:09.544
of which road you would take
to get to that goal line.

00:13:09.544 --> 00:13:14.720
So that's a kind of way
you'd try to think about it.

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:17.679
Or as I used to say about Washington,
they're firm in their opinions.

00:13:17.679 --> 00:13:19.087
It's principles they're flexible on.

00:13:19.087 --> 00:13:23.553
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I left the town so I can say that.

00:13:23.553 --> 00:13:26.302
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You mentioned your brothers.

00:13:26.302 --> 00:13:29.230
It seemed like the Emmanuel's were
destined for greatness whatever they did.

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:32.065
But what chose you-
&gt;&gt; I would not say that.

00:13:32.065 --> 00:13:33.570
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Fair enough.

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:35.094
&gt;&gt; I think it was closer to death.

00:13:35.094 --> 00:13:36.986
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So why did you choose politics?

00:13:36.986 --> 00:13:37.890
And that's where I want to go.

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:40.330
Maybe it's like death, but what drove-
&gt;&gt; No.

00:13:40.330 --> 00:13:42.519
&gt;&gt; You towards that direction?

00:13:42.519 --> 00:13:44.164
First of all,
I don't think of it as politics.

00:13:44.164 --> 00:13:45.332
I think of it as public service.

00:13:50.213 --> 00:13:51.857
So let me back up.

00:13:51.857 --> 00:13:54.008
First, it was not destined.

00:13:57.254 --> 00:13:58.156
I played soccer.

00:13:58.156 --> 00:14:00.990
I wanted to improve my soccer game,
so I took up ballet.

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:04.080
And I actually had a scholarship
to the Joffrey Ballet.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:10.044
And, much to a Jewish mother's chagrin,
I didn't take it.

00:14:10.044 --> 00:14:12.130
I went to Sarah Lawrence, and
I convinced her I'd be a dancer.

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:16.492
And the moment my parents drove off,
threw the ballet shoes.

00:14:16.492 --> 00:14:20.305
But then at college I started
studying child psychology and

00:14:20.305 --> 00:14:22.760
early childhood education.

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:28.184
Sometimes I think prepared
me to deal with politicians.

00:14:28.184 --> 00:14:30.623
In one summer, I volunteered for

00:14:30.623 --> 00:14:34.467
an interest group and
I realised this is what I love.

00:14:34.467 --> 00:14:37.340
And then my parents who were very
big about following your passion.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:42.219
My mother, but then everybody I talk
to now that I know from high school,

00:14:42.219 --> 00:14:45.192
says we always knew you were
going to go into public service.

00:14:45.192 --> 00:14:46.904
I had no idea.

00:14:46.904 --> 00:14:52.601
Now at our dinner table, which some people
refer to it as a cage match at our house.

00:14:52.601 --> 00:14:55.710
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My mother was very active in

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:59.472
the Civil Rights Movement early on and
she ran a thing in Chicago called

00:14:59.472 --> 00:15:01.932
Congress on Racial Equality
&gt;&gt; And

00:15:01.932 --> 00:15:05.773
she was basically gone and arrested
during the week, etc., not around.

00:15:05.773 --> 00:15:09.299
My father who is an immigrant
quit the AMA in 1962

00:15:09.299 --> 00:15:14.070
over National Healthcare with
barely like five words of English.

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:16.754
Not exactly a way to build a practice and

00:15:16.754 --> 00:15:21.562
lead the campaign in Chicago to
eliminate lead from household paint.

00:15:21.562 --> 00:15:22.734
And so dinner conversation.

00:15:22.734 --> 00:15:24.723
And we also grew up in
a non-nuclear family.

00:15:24.723 --> 00:15:27.060
My grandparents lived with us.

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:33.267
My father's mother, my grandmother
she lived with us for eight years.

00:15:33.267 --> 00:15:37.200
And dinner discussions was
always about current events.

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:40.493
And career-wise, I stumbled into it.

00:15:40.493 --> 00:15:44.578
I will also tell you when I got out and
I was doing community service,

00:15:44.578 --> 00:15:47.516
when I got out of college
to move back to Chicago,

00:15:47.516 --> 00:15:51.406
I wanted to work in the White House for
a President I believed in.

00:15:51.406 --> 00:15:57.526
And I got there with Clinton, and I
thought I was all over with my life at 38,

00:15:57.526 --> 00:16:01.370
and And when I was dating I told Amy,

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:04.500
I will never run for office,
I would never do that to a family.

00:16:05.810 --> 00:16:08.950
And then this seat opened up and
then I decided...

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:11.590
but I always wanted to work in
The White House, work for a president.

00:16:11.590 --> 00:16:16.430
And I believe firmly in
what I think my family,

00:16:16.430 --> 00:16:21.820
let me give you one other story which
I think is true for all of the boys.

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:28.620
So in our family room,
on the wall in the middle,

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:34.040
was a picture of my grandmother and
mother sides purse, and

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:37.830
the passport from my grandmother and
my two great aunts to come to America.

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:43.850
And on either side of that picture
which is framed of that purse and

00:16:43.850 --> 00:16:47.670
the passports were
the pictures of family members

00:16:47.670 --> 00:16:52.600
who never made it to America and
in Europe, died.

00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:55.910
And there's nothing
subtle in a Jewish home.

00:16:55.910 --> 00:17:02.030
And it was my parents reminder that
every one of us were fortunate.

00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:04.030
We were in the greatest
country in the world.

00:17:04.030 --> 00:17:07.260
And you are to do something to
give back to this great country.

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:11.790
And each of us in one way or another
through either healthcare, entertainment,

00:17:11.790 --> 00:17:15.760
or public service has tried
to give a part of their lives

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:20.900
to giving back because this is
the greatest country in the world.

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:22.830
It is still a beacon of hope.

00:17:22.830 --> 00:17:24.500
I don't care what happens.

00:17:24.500 --> 00:17:28.940
And it's still a place where people
whether they come from the Pacific,

00:17:28.940 --> 00:17:30.830
Atlantic, or the Rio.

00:17:30.830 --> 00:17:34.989
They come to give their kids a better
tomorrow than the one they left, and

00:17:34.989 --> 00:17:38.547
it is still that and always will
be if we stay true to who we are.

00:17:38.547 --> 00:17:42.073
&gt;&gt; You served two presidents in
the White House, President Clinton and

00:17:42.073 --> 00:17:43.470
President Obama.

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:45.793
What was the worst day in
the Clinton presidency for you and

00:17:45.793 --> 00:17:48.459
what did you learn from that that
helped you in your future career?

00:17:48.459 --> 00:17:53.480
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; If

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:55.128
one of you guys get your knapsacks out.

00:17:55.128 --> 00:17:57.745
Man, we're going to stay here for,
what's my worst day.

00:17:57.745 --> 00:17:59.913
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Or a slew of them, where's mine?

00:17:59.913 --> 00:18:01.707
&gt;&gt; Let's see,
let's just roll them all out.

00:18:01.707 --> 00:18:05.901
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; No, there's a personal, and

00:18:05.901 --> 00:18:09.990
then there's a, so I'll give you, my God.

00:18:09.990 --> 00:18:12.474
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This should be good.

00:18:12.474 --> 00:18:15.540
&gt;&gt; No, it's not, it's not.

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:20.700
So I told you my life long dream, right?

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:24.050
I'm going to go to, I want to work in
a White House for somebody I believe in.

00:18:24.050 --> 00:18:29.840
So Amy was still in Chicago and
we're dating.

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:34.580
She decides she will move to Washington
and join me on this, once in a lifetime,

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:36.560
I'm going to work for
a person in the White House.

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:40.210
Six months into it,
now my recommendation on the White House,

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:43.430
don't really say what you think to
the first lady, not a good idea.

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:46.370
And it was around the travel office.

00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:49.890
And I had to try to stop us
from doing the travel office.

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:50.750
We'll just leave it there.

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:58.470
So we buy a place together and
I don't have any money, but anyway,

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:03.270
she arrives the day that I was
being told that I was being fired.

00:19:03.270 --> 00:19:04.240
Not really a good thing.

00:19:04.240 --> 00:19:06.430
So she doesn't have a job,
I'm out of the job and

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:09.080
we have a mortgage that we can't afford.

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:12.120
That was kind of a low moment.

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:15.305
That's a personal low moment and
I do not know, except for

00:19:15.305 --> 00:19:19.855
my parents my father where I found, all of
the sudden, I said to the Chief of Staff,

00:19:19.855 --> 00:19:23.375
well, I'm not leaving until
the president tells me I'm gone.

00:19:25.295 --> 00:19:27.430
I don't know where I
thought I would [FOREIGN].

00:19:27.430 --> 00:19:28.247
I mean they forgot.

00:19:28.247 --> 00:19:30.241
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; No,

00:19:30.241 --> 00:19:33.084
I'm not leaving until
the President tells me I'm fired.

00:19:33.084 --> 00:19:36.020
He said what do you mean,
I said, I'm not going.

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:40.460
And I basically knew that
the President Clinton wouldn't fire me,

00:19:40.460 --> 00:19:44.928
and I don't know where I found,
I'll just say chutzpah, leave it at that.

00:19:44.928 --> 00:19:48.740
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so that was a very low point.

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:50.170
And I'll tell you another low one.

00:19:50.170 --> 00:19:53.750
So that was one low and
at the end of the day I didn't leave.

00:19:53.750 --> 00:19:57.380
And they gave me, as my concession,
so I didn't get fired obviously.

00:19:57.380 --> 00:20:01.796
And I was given NAFTA to save and
that's when we had like three votes.

00:20:01.796 --> 00:20:04.910
And so, like here, give it to Mikey.

00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:06.220
Like here, right?

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:10.030
And we ended up, I say we,
Bill Daley, and I just talked to,

00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:12.240
I'll tell you a funny
story about that too.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:15.150
So I'll come back,
that was a low point and here was another.

00:20:16.710 --> 00:20:20.190
Bruce Reed and I were responsible for
welfare reform, to get it done.

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:21.788
And if you go back then.

00:20:23.754 --> 00:20:25.630
That was a big thing about work.

00:20:25.630 --> 00:20:29.020
It was a big thing for
a Democrat to end entitlement.

00:20:30.660 --> 00:20:33.790
And a lot, and it was very contentious.

00:20:33.790 --> 00:20:35.160
And it's actually, to be honest,

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:37.520
it was exactly what you want
in a public policy debate.

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:40.240
I remember when President Clinton
walked into the cabinet room

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:45.070
we had an hour to decide to sign or
not sign, he had vetoed two prior bills.

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:47.340
The White House was split, etc.

00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:52.160
And you would actually, in your mind's
eye, would want that four hour debate

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:57.300
about something really intense, that you
were about to embark on something unknown.

00:20:57.300 --> 00:21:00.890
Was it worst than the known,
even though the known was a failure?

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:05.350
And not succeeding trapping
people in dependency, etc.

00:21:05.350 --> 00:21:07.180
But in this whole process and debate.

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:12.362
So, and he's a good friend,
so let me just say this.

00:21:12.362 --> 00:21:16.150
There was, Senator Moynihan then, the late
Senator was saying that kids are going to

00:21:16.150 --> 00:21:18.680
be on crates,
sleeping out in the middle of the night.

00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:23.092
And it was a really and contentious
debate as a country about whether, for

00:21:23.092 --> 00:21:27.523
the first time ever, we ended
a guaranteed entitlement to some minimum.

00:21:29.293 --> 00:21:37.000
And on the high holidays,
this is also the Rabbi that married us.

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:41.502
On the high holidays,
my Rabbi attacked me.

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:48.860
In his sermon for
what I was doing in my government job.

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:55.110
And I'm still a Jewish,
I still going to sit there, no.

00:21:55.110 --> 00:21:58.350
But it was actually, it was
an intense pressure because there was

00:21:58.350 --> 00:22:01.380
a lot of pressure in the way there
was a lot of people against the bill.

00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:05.470
There was a few of us for the bill and

00:22:05.470 --> 00:22:08.640
willing to take the risk of the unknown
versus the failure of the known.

00:22:09.710 --> 00:22:13.560
And thought we had done a good job in
negotiating to the president's policies.

00:22:13.560 --> 00:22:21.030
But when my rabbi from the pulpit and
I take my duty as I'm seriously.

00:22:22.410 --> 00:22:29.090
But attacked to those of us who were
advising the President on welfare reform.

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:30.625
There was not a lot of
us in the synagogue.

00:22:30.625 --> 00:22:32.250
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It was,

00:22:32.250 --> 00:22:34.140
and we weren't in the cheap seats.

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:35.830
So that was a low point.

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:39.060
I will be honest about why I
felt like I was doing what.

00:22:39.060 --> 00:22:41.890
I have a rule about politics, if I can.

00:22:45.090 --> 00:22:49.490
Because it runs against the grains of what
people want to think which is I think you

00:22:49.490 --> 00:22:53.020
have to be idealistic enough to know
why you're doing what you're doing and

00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:56.410
then ruthless enough to get it done.

00:22:56.410 --> 00:23:01.700
Sometimes my family thinks I've got
the ruthless thing down really tight.

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:03.900
Working on that, no.

00:23:03.900 --> 00:23:08.530
But I think a lot of people like to
think back, this idealistic, etc.

00:23:08.530 --> 00:23:10.490
I'm a lover of reading history.

00:23:11.640 --> 00:23:14.380
Lincoln was idealistic enough but
he was also ruthless.

00:23:15.730 --> 00:23:17.360
Okay, he didn't start to,

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:20.520
I teach eight grade,
he didn't start the war to end slavery.

00:23:22.590 --> 00:23:26.550
Now you can see and read his early
speeches where he was thinking, but

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:31.370
he knew if he got there too soon, he'd be,

00:23:31.370 --> 00:23:33.210
way ahead of where the country was and
he couldn't move it.

00:23:34.510 --> 00:23:39.040
And so he knew in his mind's
eye where he was going, but

00:23:39.040 --> 00:23:42.720
you've gotta be idealistic enough to
want to put up with this all the time and

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:46.510
then ruthless enough to get it done and
it's not a either one.

00:23:46.510 --> 00:23:50.470
And so the low point in
the Clinton was being able to

00:23:50.470 --> 00:23:53.700
be true to what I thought was right, help
a president see through what they wanted.

00:23:55.240 --> 00:23:59.530
And then be able to take your
rabbi's personal criticism

00:23:59.530 --> 00:24:02.820
in front of 1,600 other
fellow Worshippers.

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:06.532
&gt;&gt; When you took over as-
&gt;&gt; That was it for cloud services.

00:24:06.532 --> 00:24:10.220
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm never stepping

00:24:10.220 --> 00:24:12.770
foot in that synagogue again,
okay, go ahead.

00:24:12.770 --> 00:24:15.897
&gt;&gt; When you took over as Chief of Staff
in the White House, very early on,

00:24:15.897 --> 00:24:18.300
the President wanted to push for
healthcare reform.

00:24:18.300 --> 00:24:22.190
And you were one of the early opponents
within the White House and then changed.

00:24:22.190 --> 00:24:24.850
But how do you think about being-
&gt;&gt; I wouldn't say opponents but-

00:24:24.850 --> 00:24:26.210
&gt;&gt; Well, maybe you recommended against it.

00:24:26.210 --> 00:24:31.000
How do you think about being a principal
to a leader and having a robust debate,

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:31.670
and then going out and

00:24:31.670 --> 00:24:35.360
selling his policies when you
may not agree with everything?

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:39.050
&gt;&gt; Well, first of all,
you owe the President the honesty.

00:24:40.120 --> 00:24:42.991
And the good news is I worked for two
people that would never want yes people

00:24:42.991 --> 00:24:44.718
around, and boy, did I excel at that part.

00:24:44.718 --> 00:24:51.217
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So here's the thing, as Chief of Staff,

00:24:51.217 --> 00:24:55.961
your job is to help a President,
look guys,

00:24:55.961 --> 00:25:01.680
nothing is good and bad,
easy and hard, okay?

00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:05.570
It's all very complicated,
and it's not clear.

00:25:05.570 --> 00:25:09.650
And my advice to him, and
I want to be clear about what I said.

00:25:09.650 --> 00:25:13.082
I didn't say, don't do healthcare.

00:25:13.082 --> 00:25:14.880
Okay, so
I'll say what I said to the President.

00:25:14.880 --> 00:25:20.320
Okay, 100 years, Presidents have wanted
to do universal healthcare, they failed.

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:24.410
Every President has tried, has
universalized a segment of the population,

00:25:24.410 --> 00:25:29.890
not the population, MediCare, seniors,
Medicaid, poor, veterans, kid care.

00:25:29.890 --> 00:25:32.389
Nobody has succeeded at
universal healthcare.

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:37.970
My advice was, know what has come before,
so you understand this.

00:25:37.970 --> 00:25:43.820
And as you go forward, in the top
drawer of that oak desk, what's plan B?

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:46.570
because we're more likely to
get to plan B than to plan A.

00:25:46.570 --> 00:25:49.139
And if you get to plan A,
the consequences,

00:25:49.139 --> 00:25:54.001
given you're going to take on one-sixth of
the economy, is going to be unbelievable,

00:25:54.001 --> 00:25:57.489
both you trying to do something else and
to everybody else.

00:25:57.489 --> 00:26:01.880
One of the things I always say, I'm proud
to have worked for the President, but if

00:26:01.880 --> 00:26:06.606
it wasn't for the 06 and 08 House gains,
he wouldn't have gotten his agenda done.

00:26:06.606 --> 00:26:10.614
It's just not possible, and I spent a lot
of time recruiting a lot of people and

00:26:10.614 --> 00:26:13.610
convincing them this was worth doing.

00:26:13.610 --> 00:26:14.980
Okay, so that's A.

00:26:14.980 --> 00:26:19.996
B, I had to evaluate for

00:26:19.996 --> 00:26:24.936
him, which is what he wanted,
what are the trade-offs between the end,

00:26:24.936 --> 00:26:27.980
the means, and
what is the pain to pleasure?

00:26:28.980 --> 00:26:32.900
And if I didn't do that, I wouldn't
be doing my job as Chief of Staff.

00:26:32.900 --> 00:26:39.380
And once he made his decision, my role
as giving advice and counsel was over.

00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:40.520
And it was to go get the votes.

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:47.080
Now some people argued, I said to myself
another anecdote or another story.

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:48.999
I said, I'll make this feel, but

00:26:48.999 --> 00:26:52.090
I said we're not having the House
vote on Zac Permit's phone.

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:55.286
Big deal,
because that's exactly what happened.

00:26:55.286 --> 00:26:58.956
So Zac's done with the half tour section,
I'm running out of the synagogue,

00:26:58.956 --> 00:26:59.578
going back.

00:26:59.578 --> 00:27:02.071
And we're getting Bart Stupeck and

00:27:02.071 --> 00:27:06.650
the 13 Catholic Republicans on
birth control in legislation.

00:27:06.650 --> 00:27:11.290
We did a legal document, and
Bart used to work out with me, etc.

00:27:11.290 --> 00:27:13.900
And so, my only point to him was,

00:27:13.900 --> 00:27:17.480
understand what it's going to take
politically to get this done,

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:20.540
and if you get it done, what
are the consequences of getting it done?

00:27:22.130 --> 00:27:24.727
And there's good things policy wise,
and there's challenges politically.

00:27:26.490 --> 00:27:31.570
And after healthcare, it was Dodd Frank,

00:27:32.670 --> 00:27:36.359
but I'll give you the rest of
the Presidency was executive orders.

00:27:38.650 --> 00:27:41.050
You didn't replenish the political bank,
you drained it.

00:27:42.070 --> 00:27:46.845
And if you were a democratic President
with a great vision of what government can

00:27:46.845 --> 00:27:49.208
do and what you want to do, understood.

00:27:49.208 --> 00:27:52.300
And that was my job was to say what
I believe, and I believe that.

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:56.260
And I'm happy he did it, I think there's
been great things that's gotten done.

00:27:56.260 --> 00:28:00.260
But if I had not done that,
then all this notion,

00:28:00.260 --> 00:28:03.120
go into that Oval Office and
tell him the truth.

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:03.650
Okay, I did.

00:28:05.280 --> 00:28:05.970
That's what I did.

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:09.650
And I believe still to today
you have to evaluate equities.

00:28:09.650 --> 00:28:13.450
And it's not A to B,
there's a lot of complications to it.

00:28:14.520 --> 00:28:21.400
And you give the President advice, and
he makes or she makes the decision.

00:28:21.400 --> 00:28:27.049
And then your job as a staff person is to
execute it, and that's exactly what I did.

00:28:27.049 --> 00:28:30.159
&gt;&gt; So in 2011, you went from being that
executive agent of the President to

00:28:30.159 --> 00:28:32.570
actually being the principal yourself.

00:28:32.570 --> 00:28:35.350
And what kind of leadership
changes did you have to make, or

00:28:35.350 --> 00:28:38.440
what kind of things as a leader
did you have to shift about your

00:28:38.440 --> 00:28:40.880
engagement with your staff to
actually be the person in charge?

00:28:43.190 --> 00:28:44.875
&gt;&gt; Thank God you're not
interviewing the staff.

00:28:44.875 --> 00:28:51.006
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So there's a couple things.

00:28:51.006 --> 00:28:52.266
Well, first of all,

00:28:52.266 --> 00:28:56.130
I think there are three qualities
a chief executive has to exude.

00:28:57.710 --> 00:29:01.960
This is true for Presidents, it's true for
a mayor, true for governor, but

00:29:01.960 --> 00:29:04.450
I can't say it for the private sector.

00:29:04.450 --> 00:29:08.400
But I can say in the public sector,
you always have to communicate strength,

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:09.410
confidence, and optimism.

00:29:11.800 --> 00:29:18.326
Nobody goes for the weaker, not sure,
insecure, or a dark person, or whatever.

00:29:18.326 --> 00:29:24.800
So those are qualities, but
remember, unlike an elected official

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:28.820
who's a legislator, as a Congressman,
you have to represent people's views.

00:29:29.980 --> 00:29:34.367
As a mayor or as a president,
they give you that

00:29:34.367 --> 00:29:39.170
responsibility to take care of business,

00:29:39.170 --> 00:29:44.460
not to represent them, but it's
a different role and a different function.

00:29:45.870 --> 00:29:51.280
And I'd run for office before, been
a Chief of Staff to run a White House,

00:29:52.400 --> 00:29:57.100
and mayor though, it is a transition

00:29:57.100 --> 00:30:02.280
from being an agent for somebody else
to being the principal for your city.

00:30:02.280 --> 00:30:05.890
Totally, it is a different voice and
a different role.

00:30:05.890 --> 00:30:09.500
But I've been in office before and so
that, and one level is not different.

00:30:09.500 --> 00:30:15.410
On the other hand, there are things I do
when I was a Congressman to Chief of Staff

00:30:15.410 --> 00:30:19.330
that I continue as mayor, which are weekly
reports to keep tabs of what I need going.

00:30:20.600 --> 00:30:24.990
And making sure that people are executing
on a certain mission, like this Thursday,

00:30:24.990 --> 00:30:26.570
I'm giving a major address.

00:30:26.570 --> 00:30:29.479
It's the five year anniversary of
the first address that I gave on

00:30:29.479 --> 00:30:33.000
infrastructure and
laying out our plan from our

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:37.190
runways to our rail to our roads and
what we're doing and stuff like that.

00:30:37.190 --> 00:30:43.326
But also understanding what the staff's
job is versus what I'm trying to do.

00:30:43.326 --> 00:30:44.060
Does that make sense?

00:30:44.060 --> 00:30:44.775
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely, thank you.

00:30:44.775 --> 00:30:45.631
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:30:45.631 --> 00:30:46.166
&gt;&gt; Last year-
&gt;&gt; Good,

00:30:46.166 --> 00:30:47.412
because I had no idea what I was saying.

00:30:47.412 --> 00:30:50.236
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] You're a politician,
so you're doing a very good job.

00:30:50.236 --> 00:30:54.447
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You have a bad view of what public

00:30:54.447 --> 00:30:55.680
service is about.

00:30:55.680 --> 00:31:00.030
&gt;&gt; Last year, your approval ratings hit an
all time low, but they've since recovered.

00:31:00.030 --> 00:31:03.160
So as a leader who's at the whims
of the public to some extent,

00:31:03.160 --> 00:31:06.520
how do you get through those difficult
times and then recover as you have?

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:07.612
&gt;&gt; Find a different rabbi.

00:31:07.612 --> 00:31:11.381
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:31:11.381 --> 00:31:14.769
&gt;&gt; I think, Look,

00:31:18.426 --> 00:31:21.910
There's a difference,
also it's not always consistent.

00:31:21.910 --> 00:31:26.980
So what I mean by how a President or
how, if you are in the second term

00:31:26.980 --> 00:31:29.670
versus the first term where you are,
that will kind of weigh different.

00:31:30.840 --> 00:31:32.500
I always kind of remind myself or

00:31:32.500 --> 00:31:37.500
try to, which is the decisions we
do today and I tell my staff and

00:31:37.500 --> 00:31:41.650
more importantly the Cabinet,
what we're doing today for the next two,

00:31:41.650 --> 00:31:45.750
three years will determine what Chicago
will be like for the next 20 to 30 years.

00:31:45.750 --> 00:31:50.677
And you've gotta be willing
to take certain political

00:31:50.677 --> 00:31:55.935
points pressure today that's
worthwhile down the road.

00:31:55.935 --> 00:31:58.778
So I have certain things I learned
out of Clinton and Obama and

00:31:58.778 --> 00:32:03.800
I This is a bad example, but
it's one to illustrate the point.

00:32:04.860 --> 00:32:10.470
So we're embarking,
we did it five years ago,

00:32:10.470 --> 00:32:14.860
I've raised water rates 25%, 15%,
15%, 15% and then indexed them so

00:32:14.860 --> 00:32:17.370
nobody has to go back and
to this vote again on raising water rates.

00:32:17.370 --> 00:32:21.300
And we're replacing 900
miles of water pipe.

00:32:21.300 --> 00:32:27.230
Everything that's a hundred years or older
in the city will be replaced by 2019.

00:32:27.230 --> 00:32:29.840
Every 670 miles of sewer
will be the relined,

00:32:29.840 --> 00:32:31.470
replaced everything 100 years or older.

00:32:31.470 --> 00:32:36.767
We're replacing 167,000 catch basins and
the two largest water

00:32:36.767 --> 00:32:42.147
filtration plants in the United States
of America, built top to bottom,

00:32:42.147 --> 00:32:47.988
and I indexed it, and the reason is,
once I got that vote, it passed 50 to 0.

00:32:50.154 --> 00:32:52.506
But, I didn't want another Mayor,
or in city council,

00:32:52.506 --> 00:32:55.520
to have to go through the politics
of raising rates again.

00:32:55.520 --> 00:32:58.060
So it's now indexed
once we're done at the.

00:32:58.060 --> 00:33:02.200
You will continue to fund so the
infrastructure continues to go forward,

00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:02.780
straightforward.

00:33:02.780 --> 00:33:07.500
I didn't had think somebody should go back
and try to do once I did those politics.

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:09.680
And a certain things that what we're
doing on the rail, stuff etc.,

00:33:09.680 --> 00:33:13.500
take care of the politics, get it out.

00:33:13.500 --> 00:33:15.230
Does that make sense,
what I'm trying to say?

00:33:15.230 --> 00:33:20.590
And so in that sense,
I'm trying to make decisions

00:33:20.590 --> 00:33:25.750
that I think over the time will stand
the test of time for generations to come.

00:33:25.750 --> 00:33:29.160
And that, and probably the biggest
one there was a bad example, but

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:29.819
now I'm thinking about it.

00:33:31.210 --> 00:33:33.591
I ran on the fact that Chicago
had the shortest school day and

00:33:33.591 --> 00:33:36.221
the shortest school year, and
I was going to do something about it.

00:33:38.113 --> 00:33:41.040
And I was not going to let our
kids be dead last on time.

00:33:41.040 --> 00:33:43.530
And I knew that time in class mattered.

00:33:43.530 --> 00:33:47.560
And when it came to that strike, could I
solve the strike, or not had a strike,

00:33:47.560 --> 00:33:49.920
if I threw the towel in and said,
wow, this is harder than I thought?

00:33:52.010 --> 00:33:53.020
Yes.

00:33:53.020 --> 00:33:55.490
Do I think another mayor
after me will go back and

00:33:55.490 --> 00:33:57.020
say it like you go back to
the shortest school day?

00:33:57.020 --> 00:33:59.890
No, I think they're going to
say I'm glad that hour and

00:33:59.890 --> 00:34:02.580
fifteen minutes was added and
now I don't have to debate it anymore.

00:34:04.110 --> 00:34:08.420
And so you've got to be,
as a person, comfortable with that

00:34:08.420 --> 00:34:14.130
sometimes you're going to
take it in the shorts.

00:34:14.130 --> 00:34:16.290
But its worth your taking the politics.

00:34:16.290 --> 00:34:20.260
Now, here's the deal I took a week
strike in the city which I hadn't,

00:34:20.260 --> 00:34:20.990
I'm not proud of it.

00:34:22.050 --> 00:34:25.600
But was it worth the hour and
15 minutes of everyday for every child and

00:34:25.600 --> 00:34:26.360
two weeks every year?

00:34:26.360 --> 00:34:31.080
The fact that your eighth graders
now lead the country in math games.

00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:34.680
Your fourth graders are third in
reading gains in the United States.

00:34:34.680 --> 00:34:37.850
Your graduation is triple rate of
the United States of America for

00:34:37.850 --> 00:34:39.160
every year for the last four years.

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:43.790
Yeah, it's worth it, so
I'll take a week's strike.

00:34:43.790 --> 00:34:49.350
And our kids our now leading the country
in educational gains, totally worth it.

00:34:49.350 --> 00:34:51.920
And I would do it again,
I would like not to.

00:34:53.638 --> 00:35:01.760
But there are certain that you have to
take the political hit short term etc.

00:35:01.760 --> 00:35:06.630
If you think the long term gain for
other people is worth that.

00:35:06.630 --> 00:35:13.030
And that was a same thing in
a different venue that Presidents when

00:35:13.030 --> 00:35:18.240
they make tough calls in the chance of
Obama and healthcare, that's worth doing.

00:35:19.290 --> 00:35:21.210
And you think it's worth the gain.

00:35:21.210 --> 00:35:23.540
And you're willing to take
the political hit and spend it.

00:35:23.540 --> 00:35:27.600
I would also,
nothing's stagnant in politics.

00:35:27.600 --> 00:35:28.580
It's a dynamic thing.

00:35:30.110 --> 00:35:34.150
And yes, we're in a better position, but
you go through those periods of times.

00:35:34.150 --> 00:35:36.950
And hopefully,
you learn something out of them.

00:35:36.950 --> 00:35:38.480
&gt;&gt; One of the tensions, I think,

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:43.490
a lot of startups face is 20th
century regulatory environment.

00:35:43.490 --> 00:35:44.660
The 20th century regulatory environment.

00:35:44.660 --> 00:35:47.620
So, Uber tried to come to Chicago,
they had some challenges there.

00:35:47.620 --> 00:35:53.110
Austin has done some things to maybe
exclude Air Bnb, Uber and Lyft, how do

00:35:53.110 --> 00:35:57.660
you think about integrating new technology
and cutting edge things when it comes to

00:35:57.660 --> 00:36:01.370
actually, also maintaining the regulations
that keep your city safe or whatever?

00:36:01.370 --> 00:36:03.770
How do you blend those two things?

00:36:03.770 --> 00:36:07.220
&gt;&gt; Well, we just went through this with
Uber, who I also met with this morning, so

00:36:07.220 --> 00:36:09.670
I'll give you an example.

00:36:09.670 --> 00:36:15.870
The hotel, so when I became Mayor, the
hotel industry we had 39 million visitors.

00:36:15.870 --> 00:36:20.340
We now have 54 million visitors and
it's been an incredible growth

00:36:20.340 --> 00:36:24.580
both in tourism beyond business
travelling to tourist and

00:36:24.580 --> 00:36:28.480
we are really marketing and the hotel
industry loves what's happening and

00:36:28.480 --> 00:36:31.750
we've redone Macormic place,
convention business, blah blah blah.

00:36:32.880 --> 00:36:36.835
There was an official business term,
blah, blah, blah.

00:36:36.835 --> 00:36:38.260
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So that goes forward.

00:36:38.260 --> 00:36:45.200
So Airbnb comes and I said to the hotel
industry, who's very supportive.

00:36:45.200 --> 00:36:45.840
Look, they're here.

00:36:47.280 --> 00:36:48.780
We're not keeping them out.

00:36:48.780 --> 00:36:51.550
The question is what are the rules
they're going to operate under?

00:36:51.550 --> 00:36:54.520
So we did something which
I'm really proud of.

00:36:54.520 --> 00:36:58.875
Beyond they paying a hotel taxes
that's AirBNB, I put a 4% surtax for

00:36:58.875 --> 00:36:59.900
homelessness.

00:37:01.470 --> 00:37:05.872
And we now raised 2.5 to $3
million now just started but

00:37:05.872 --> 00:37:09.568
beyond the 16% tax that
gives all hotels pay and

00:37:09.568 --> 00:37:14.830
AirBNB pays there's a 4% surtax just for
homeless services.

00:37:14.830 --> 00:37:20.120
And we've discussed today something new
which no other city's going to do which

00:37:20.120 --> 00:37:24.130
we're going to try and I can't say it
now because I gotta work something out.

00:37:24.130 --> 00:37:26.355
But I'm confident I will,
you'll be the first to know.

00:37:26.355 --> 00:37:31.220
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But you've gotta be able to see what

00:37:32.520 --> 00:37:33.659
are the fundamentals.

00:37:34.850 --> 00:37:36.500
And then what's your goal out of it.

00:37:36.500 --> 00:37:40.925
Nobody in a negotiation,
nobody ever signs on to a loss.

00:37:43.330 --> 00:37:46.220
Everything I always say to our
team before we go into anything.

00:37:46.220 --> 00:37:50.500
So that was like true when the hotel
industry wanted to throttle,

00:37:52.480 --> 00:37:56.130
and I said okay,
they want to throttle AirBnB.

00:37:56.130 --> 00:38:00.710
There are other people, nobody signs
on to a loss, nobody willingly.

00:38:00.710 --> 00:38:05.530
And so the question is, as I always
say to my team what's their wins?

00:38:07.250 --> 00:38:11.540
I always, before a negotiation, draw out
on a piece of paper their wins, my wins.

00:38:13.460 --> 00:38:13.960
Always.

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:18.203
Now, can I give them what
I think are their wins?

00:38:21.896 --> 00:38:25.176
Can they give me my wins?

00:38:25.176 --> 00:38:31.730
And then, how close are we to that
kind of ideal paper at the beginning?

00:38:31.730 --> 00:38:32.620
Then when we get to the end.

00:38:33.880 --> 00:38:37.930
Usually people like to walk or
make [INAUDIBLE].

00:38:37.930 --> 00:38:39.790
We did it recently with the teachers.

00:38:39.790 --> 00:38:43.660
There are things in there, the teachers
can claim to their members, they won.

00:38:43.660 --> 00:38:44.350
Fair and square.

00:38:44.350 --> 00:38:45.370
Not a problem.

00:38:45.370 --> 00:38:48.240
There's things we won, fair and square.

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:51.900
Otherwise it's not a very good
negotiations or contract.

00:38:51.900 --> 00:38:54.070
If you try to make the other side lose.

00:38:54.070 --> 00:38:58.440
It's not usually good way to get a deal
done, and they won't sign on to it,

00:38:58.440 --> 00:39:01.270
and, if they do sign on to it,
it's because you crushed them, and,

00:39:01.270 --> 00:39:02.600
trust me,
they're going to come back and get you.

00:39:03.970 --> 00:39:05.710
What goes around comes around.

00:39:05.710 --> 00:39:06.880
So, I always try to start,

00:39:06.880 --> 00:39:11.420
when we negotiate something,
sit at a table with my staff, and says,

00:39:11.420 --> 00:39:14.770
okay, write out to me their wins, and
the ones we can afford to give them.

00:39:16.700 --> 00:39:19.150
Now right out my wins that
they can afford to give me.

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:23.820
And then let's try to keep that
as our north star in this deal.

00:39:25.810 --> 00:39:29.740
But my biggest problem, for
me, is we kind of know it so

00:39:29.740 --> 00:39:30.968
let's just get to it already.

00:39:30.968 --> 00:39:33.680
And usually like
negotiations have their own

00:39:33.680 --> 00:39:36.070
theater performance before you get there.

00:39:36.070 --> 00:39:36.930
And I'm not one for that.

00:39:39.170 --> 00:39:41.215
Patience is not one of my strong suits.

00:39:41.215 --> 00:39:45.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Following the 2016 election,
democrats are in a tough spot.

00:39:45.290 --> 00:39:46.100
&gt;&gt; You noticed?

00:39:46.100 --> 00:39:46.800
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; They're a minority

00:39:46.800 --> 00:39:48.320
across the federal government.

00:39:48.320 --> 00:39:48.990
&gt;&gt; Really?

00:39:48.990 --> 00:39:53.090
&gt;&gt; In 2018, they'll have to
defend 25 of the 33 center races.

00:39:53.090 --> 00:39:54.200
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; You've

00:39:54.200 --> 00:39:56.921
lost over 1,000 state
legislatives since 2009.

00:39:56.921 --> 00:39:57.900
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:39:57.900 --> 00:40:00.226
&gt;&gt; But in 2006, you As the GCCC chairman,

00:40:00.226 --> 00:40:03.262
who won the House, what do the have
to do to regain their [CROSSTALK]

00:40:03.262 --> 00:40:04.076
&gt;&gt; Are you my rabbi?

00:40:04.076 --> 00:40:05.410
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I could be.

00:40:05.410 --> 00:40:06.430
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:40:06.430 --> 00:40:06.930
Really.

00:40:08.630 --> 00:40:09.692
Well, here look.

00:40:12.914 --> 00:40:16.821
I probably shouldn't be too honest.

00:40:16.821 --> 00:40:18.052
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:40:20.010 --> 00:40:25.132
So look, There's a couple things.

00:40:25.132 --> 00:40:30.564
No, this has not been 2016 on
the house and state house.

00:40:30.564 --> 00:40:35.734
2016 now, Democrats are at the lowest
level since 1928 in the House

00:40:35.734 --> 00:40:41.246
of Representatives, and the lowest
levels since 1925 In the state houses.

00:40:44.436 --> 00:40:46.230
Not really good, okay?

00:40:46.230 --> 00:40:49.580
I know you didn't pay me the big bucks
to come here and tell you that, but

00:40:49.580 --> 00:40:53.250
it is hard to imagine it getting lower.

00:40:53.250 --> 00:40:54.890
So there's a lot of different problems.

00:40:54.890 --> 00:40:57.649
One, you gotta go out and
create a farm team.

00:40:57.649 --> 00:41:03.640
In 06 and 08, when I and everybody goes,
that’s when we took back the house.

00:41:03.640 --> 00:41:06.690
I got a lot of crap for
recruiting Iraq war vets,

00:41:06.690 --> 00:41:11.170
football players, sheriffs,
and business people.

00:41:11.170 --> 00:41:13.280
Well I said they're running
in a Republican districts.

00:41:14.710 --> 00:41:16.590
I wanted to take cultural
issues off the table and

00:41:16.590 --> 00:41:17.950
I wanted to present economic issues.

00:41:19.550 --> 00:41:21.770
You know, we as Democrats like
to walk around like, deal.

00:41:21.770 --> 00:41:23.730
No, you got to be ruthless enough.

00:41:23.730 --> 00:41:26.170
We recruited people who
matched the district.

00:41:26.170 --> 00:41:27.990
If you're running in
a Republican district,

00:41:27.990 --> 00:41:30.700
you got to get at somebody who
can win in a Republican district.

00:41:32.830 --> 00:41:34.440
Winning's everything.

00:41:34.440 --> 00:41:37.320
You don't win,
you can't make the public policy.

00:41:38.360 --> 00:41:43.090
I say that because it is hard for people
in our party to accept that principle.

00:41:43.090 --> 00:41:46.912
Sometimes you just gotta win, okay.

00:41:46.912 --> 00:41:52.340
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:52.340 --> 00:41:54.060
&gt;&gt; Our party likes to be right,

00:41:55.690 --> 00:41:56.510
even if they lose.

00:41:57.580 --> 00:41:59.490
I don't go to moral victory speeches.

00:41:59.490 --> 00:42:03.130
I can't stand them I've
never lost an election.

00:42:04.130 --> 00:42:05.020
It's about winning.

00:42:05.020 --> 00:42:08.620
Because if you win, you then have the
power to go do what you need to get done.

00:42:08.620 --> 00:42:12.700
If you lose, you can write
this book about what happened.

00:42:12.700 --> 00:42:14.270
Great, that's really exciting.

00:42:16.430 --> 00:42:18.590
And my view is, right now, number one.

00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:21.000
Get on top of redistricting.

00:42:23.180 --> 00:42:26.720
I want it all handled by courts and
commissions, get the state legislatures

00:42:26.720 --> 00:42:30.690
out of that, because we have not
done it the other way and they have.

00:42:31.730 --> 00:42:33.070
Gotta change that.

00:42:33.070 --> 00:42:34.326
Second, go get a firm team.

00:42:34.326 --> 00:42:38.750
Third, in this effort,

00:42:38.750 --> 00:42:42.610
stop arguing about Democrats love
doing a firing squad in the circle.

00:42:42.610 --> 00:42:43.110
Stop it.

00:42:44.150 --> 00:42:45.130
Okay?

00:42:45.130 --> 00:42:46.580
They're too moderate.

00:42:46.580 --> 00:42:48.050
Forget about it.

00:42:49.100 --> 00:42:52.560
This guy and these people are about
to do something on the tax code,

00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:56.040
the regulatory environment, and
things that are more threatening

00:42:56.040 --> 00:42:58.660
than what a fellow Democrat may
slightly disagree with you on.

00:43:00.290 --> 00:43:01.690
Stop it.

00:43:01.690 --> 00:43:03.440
We're not strong enough to do that.

00:43:03.440 --> 00:43:06.360
And then you gotta pick which
ones you're going to fight about.

00:43:06.360 --> 00:43:07.910
Not every pitch has to be swung at.

00:43:08.950 --> 00:43:11.060
I only use that because
of the Chicago Cubs.

00:43:11.060 --> 00:43:15.160
I don't know anything about baseball,
but I know that's what I say, okay.

00:43:15.160 --> 00:43:17.730
We don't have the power to
swing at everything, so

00:43:17.730 --> 00:43:20.190
you have to pick what is essential.

00:43:20.190 --> 00:43:22.020
Now what we do know I would say also.

00:43:23.580 --> 00:43:28.650
Time is not the incumbent party's friend,
time is the opposition's friend.

00:43:28.650 --> 00:43:32.136
Slow, go slow.

00:43:32.136 --> 00:43:37.255
They want to rush,
we want to go slow, real slow.

00:43:37.255 --> 00:43:40.510
B, wedge.

00:43:40.510 --> 00:43:43.000
Wherever there's a disagreement
among Republicans, I'm for

00:43:43.000 --> 00:43:44.575
one of those disagreements.

00:43:44.575 --> 00:43:48.760
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm all for it, okay?

00:43:48.760 --> 00:43:51.630
&gt;&gt; Fair enough.

00:43:51.630 --> 00:43:52.610
&gt;&gt; Presidents want Russia.

00:43:52.610 --> 00:43:54.890
I'm with John McCain and Lindsey Graham.

00:43:54.890 --> 00:43:55.711
I'm for NATO.

00:43:55.711 --> 00:43:56.818
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:43:56.818 --> 00:43:58.300
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Why?

00:43:58.300 --> 00:43:58.880
Wedges.

00:43:58.880 --> 00:44:00.260
Schisms have to be wedges.

00:44:00.260 --> 00:44:02.130
Wedges has to be divides and divisions.

00:44:03.880 --> 00:44:05.100
And third, let me be real clear.

00:44:06.670 --> 00:44:09.490
We gotta lower the president.

00:44:09.490 --> 00:44:10.560
Why?

00:44:10.560 --> 00:44:17.500
Because they are strong enough to get
them to us, we're not strong enough.

00:44:17.500 --> 00:44:20.090
And that's my kind of take on this and
that is how you built.

00:44:20.090 --> 00:44:24.450
Now, you also got to know you
can't beat something with nothing,

00:44:24.450 --> 00:44:26.910
that was Bill Clinton's
always lesson to me.

00:44:26.910 --> 00:44:28.560
You have to have an alternative.

00:44:28.560 --> 00:44:33.450
So when I was in Congress and head of the
DZZZ I wrote a book with Bruce Reed about

00:44:34.470 --> 00:44:37.040
a set of policy ideas for
the Democratic party.

00:44:38.190 --> 00:44:39.740
You have to give people your vision.

00:44:42.420 --> 00:44:46.350
So those are kind of the rules of
what I would say to take part and

00:44:46.350 --> 00:44:47.600
I would also be honest.

00:44:47.600 --> 00:44:50.610
It took us a long time to get this low and
it ain't going to happen in 2018.

00:44:50.610 --> 00:44:52.980
Take a chopper man.

00:44:52.980 --> 00:44:54.800
This is what you going to be and
miss for the long hole.

00:44:56.020 --> 00:44:59.880
And if you think it could be a quick
turn around like that it's not.

00:44:59.880 --> 00:45:01.110
You have to be part of this for

00:45:01.110 --> 00:45:05.340
a long hall coz our ideas
are our ideas like theirs.

00:45:05.340 --> 00:45:07.870
They didn't get to this
point just overnight so

00:45:07.870 --> 00:45:08.790
things are going to be like this.

00:45:08.790 --> 00:45:11.980
You are going to have a success here,
you are going to have a success there, and

00:45:11.980 --> 00:45:14.850
then you build the critical mass and
bet if it is worth fighting for.

00:45:14.850 --> 00:45:16.760
And I think this country
is worth fighting for.

00:45:16.760 --> 00:45:19.922
&gt;&gt; I have one last question before
going to the Q and A with the audience.

00:45:19.922 --> 00:45:20.884
&gt;&gt; I have two last answers.

00:45:20.884 --> 00:45:23.050
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Fantastic, hopefully.

00:45:23.050 --> 00:45:25.663
Who is the republican you most respected
in Washington when you were there?

00:45:29.423 --> 00:45:33.485
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Who's

00:45:33.485 --> 00:45:38.773
the Republican I'm most respected?

00:45:38.773 --> 00:45:39.450
So here's the thing.

00:45:40.460 --> 00:45:49.560
In 08, I handled the negotiations around
the debate between Obama, the structure.

00:45:49.560 --> 00:45:51.256
Lindsey Graham did for the other side.

00:45:51.256 --> 00:45:57.910
And Lindsey and I ended up developing

00:45:57.910 --> 00:46:03.710
a friendship that goes beyond.

00:46:03.710 --> 00:46:06.660
We used to have dinner,
and stuff like that.

00:46:06.660 --> 00:46:07.940
Not that that we just have dinner.

00:46:07.940 --> 00:46:10.555
But it was, I even picked up the tab, no.

00:46:10.555 --> 00:46:16.050
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But anyways, so and I say that because

00:46:16.050 --> 00:46:21.866
he and I at the end of the day with Carl
Levin negotiated an agreement to handle

00:46:21.866 --> 00:46:27.350
the trials for terrorist and

00:46:27.350 --> 00:46:30.710
a structure in which you would put
Republican votes to close Guantanamo.

00:46:32.220 --> 00:46:36.520
And he was willing to take
a political risk to get that done.

00:46:36.520 --> 00:46:39.356
Now, what is, let me stop right there,
we always say a political.

00:46:39.356 --> 00:46:43.805
A political risk means you're willing to
do something where you spend political

00:46:43.805 --> 00:46:46.488
capital and
people that are friends and family,

00:46:46.488 --> 00:46:50.560
are going to hate you at the end of it but
you think it's the right thing to do.

00:46:50.560 --> 00:46:55.690
And I respected Lindsay in the end of
the day because on that one deal that

00:46:55.690 --> 00:47:00.980
we worked out the conference table in
the Chief of Staff's office Carl Levin,

00:47:00.980 --> 00:47:05.530
he and I over lunch had worked through the
agreements to both achieve the president's

00:47:05.530 --> 00:47:07.810
goal of shutting down Guantanamo.

00:47:07.810 --> 00:47:12.540
Lindsay's goal of what cases were
military trial versus civilian.

00:47:12.540 --> 00:47:14.270
That was the big issue then.

00:47:14.270 --> 00:47:16.100
And a set of protocols
they could work through.

00:47:16.100 --> 00:47:19.287
And he was willing to take
the risk to come out and

00:47:19.287 --> 00:47:21.524
work with Obama and stand there.

00:47:21.524 --> 00:47:25.154
And I think,
I'm trying to separate both respect and

00:47:25.154 --> 00:47:30.411
the fact that we got along, which
probably influenced the respect part too.

00:47:30.411 --> 00:47:32.498
The other person I would say
is Senator Susan Collins.

00:47:36.371 --> 00:47:40.354
And probably Bob Gates,
the Secretary of Defense.

00:47:40.354 --> 00:47:42.332
Those would be the three.

00:47:44.334 --> 00:47:45.231
Thank you so much.

00:47:45.231 --> 00:47:46.887
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:47:46.887 --> 00:47:49.167
&gt;&gt; Any questions from the audience?

00:47:49.167 --> 00:47:50.757
&gt;&gt; There, do I call people or you?

00:47:50.757 --> 00:47:53.650
&gt;&gt; So
we got some ushers back there that will-

00:47:53.650 --> 00:47:54.820
&gt;&gt; Just like certain, okay.

00:47:56.260 --> 00:47:57.928
Thank you for being here.

00:47:57.928 --> 00:48:00.014
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; I was actually at your 2011

00:48:00.014 --> 00:48:05.190
inauguration, and remember how great
Chicago felt to live in at that moment.

00:48:05.190 --> 00:48:07.807
&gt;&gt; Come on back.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:48:07.807 --> 00:48:09.315
&gt;&gt; It feels like the political moment now

00:48:09.315 --> 00:48:11.870
is a little different, and
you addressed this on a macro level.

00:48:11.870 --> 00:48:16.393
But I'm curious, having faced your
own populist challenge in 2015,

00:48:16.393 --> 00:48:19.480
what strategies you think work for
candidates, or

00:48:19.480 --> 00:48:24.098
for even people who feel like they're
fighting against a wave of populism.

00:48:24.098 --> 00:48:28.578
&gt;&gt; Because I want to take more questions,
I don't want to argue out what

00:48:28.578 --> 00:48:33.305
populism means, because that would
be the definitional point here.

00:48:33.305 --> 00:48:37.085
Let me say, so I was up for

00:48:37.085 --> 00:48:41.587
re-election in 2015 and

00:48:41.587 --> 00:48:47.538
I told President Clinton in 2014.

00:48:47.538 --> 00:48:51.433
I said, I'm just telling you right now,
this is a funky moment.

00:48:51.433 --> 00:48:52.962
I said I've never seen anything like this.

00:48:52.962 --> 00:48:57.504
Now, let me say, I have a picture
in my office of the first meeting,

00:48:57.504 --> 00:49:00.640
on the first day at 9 AM
with President Obama.

00:49:00.640 --> 00:49:03.946
And you know because the fire's
in the background, it's winter,

00:49:03.946 --> 00:49:05.112
it's January 20th.

00:49:05.112 --> 00:49:09.255
If I told him,
in the darkest moments of the recession,

00:49:09.255 --> 00:49:14.281
the auto industry about to collapse,
financial sector offers up,

00:49:14.281 --> 00:49:20.123
you're going to have unemployment from
10 some odd percent down to 4.9%.

00:49:20.123 --> 00:49:23.710
Health care will basically be universal.

00:49:25.000 --> 00:49:30.183
Energy costs and interest rates will be
probably at the lowest levels in decades.

00:49:30.183 --> 00:49:32.790
Will people be content,
or really honked off?

00:49:34.090 --> 00:49:36.483
They would have gone with content,
you know?

00:49:36.483 --> 00:49:40.599
And they are honked off,

00:49:40.599 --> 00:49:44.728
like really honked off.

00:49:44.728 --> 00:49:45.602
And 4.9%, 4.8% unemployment.

00:49:45.602 --> 00:49:49.702
Now I understand and I would argue
two points, there are two things that

00:49:49.702 --> 00:49:53.402
are really driving us and
I don't see them out being discussed.

00:49:53.402 --> 00:49:58.320
They're not a part of what I think is
what, quote unquote, driving the populism.

00:49:58.320 --> 00:49:59.926
Two things I would say to you.

00:49:59.926 --> 00:50:04.790
One, people are more upset at the politics
than they are at the economics right now.

00:50:06.720 --> 00:50:08.460
Everybody's talking about the economic.

00:50:08.460 --> 00:50:12.150
People are really frustrated with
the politics and the dysfunction.

00:50:12.150 --> 00:50:15.740
Not just here, look at France,
look at what's going on.

00:50:15.740 --> 00:50:17.036
The second, what's driving that?

00:50:17.036 --> 00:50:24.968
I also believe people have made a huge
compromises about their position today.

00:50:24.968 --> 00:50:30.289
And what I mean by that is, over the last
20 years their spouses went to work, your

00:50:30.289 --> 00:50:36.850
mother-in-law's have moved in, the kids
graduate college, they're coming home.

00:50:36.850 --> 00:50:38.500
They've kind of made
all those compromises,

00:50:38.500 --> 00:50:41.420
adjustments to kind of
hold it all together.

00:50:41.420 --> 00:50:46.280
What is really driving people's angst,
is their kids.

00:50:46.280 --> 00:50:50.011
They think that even if they send them to
college, that's not a guarantee anymore,

00:50:50.011 --> 00:50:52.600
and they have to go to the poorhouse
just to get them there.

00:50:53.670 --> 00:50:59.284
And they don't see the political
system addressing the future better.

00:50:59.284 --> 00:51:02.580
They know the future's going to
be rough for their kids.

00:51:03.800 --> 00:51:06.610
And what they're worried about is the
dysfunction of the system is not getting

00:51:06.610 --> 00:51:10.440
its stuff together to make that
future better rather than worse.

00:51:10.440 --> 00:51:13.220
And I think that's where the populism,
that's where the anger,

00:51:13.220 --> 00:51:14.730
that's where the frustration is.

00:51:14.730 --> 00:51:18.477
It's not about this class,
and I'm not saying for

00:51:18.477 --> 00:51:23.272
the last ten years this staticness
around middle class incomes,

00:51:23.272 --> 00:51:27.388
and the loss pre-recession
versus post-recession.

00:51:27.388 --> 00:51:31.365
But I actually believe the biggest
angst that's driving people is around

00:51:31.365 --> 00:51:33.546
a dysfunction in the political system,

00:51:33.546 --> 00:51:37.543
driven mainly by their fear about
tomorrow will not be better than today.

00:51:37.543 --> 00:51:40.843
And that's the first time that's
ever happened in America, and

00:51:40.843 --> 00:51:43.250
that's my kind of take on it.

00:51:43.250 --> 00:51:43.985
Did that help?

00:51:43.985 --> 00:51:44.820
Okay.

00:51:45.830 --> 00:51:47.622
Come back to Chicago
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:51:47.622 --> 00:51:49.180
&gt;&gt; It's raining here too often.

00:51:50.550 --> 00:51:52.840
You gotta pick on people,
I'm just a little bit [INAUDIBLE].

00:51:52.840 --> 00:51:54.802
&gt;&gt; Mayor Immanuel,
thank you for coming here.

00:51:54.802 --> 00:51:58.040
My name's George, I am a second year
MBA student from St. Louis, Missouri.

00:51:58.040 --> 00:52:02.391
And I am interested in understanding, what
are some specific policy areas that you're

00:52:02.391 --> 00:52:04.704
excited to work with
a new administration on?

00:52:04.704 --> 00:52:14.704
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:52:16.532 --> 00:52:19.931
&gt;&gt; Reversing their policies and
immigration, reversing their tax policy.

00:52:19.931 --> 00:52:20.636
No.

00:52:20.636 --> 00:52:25.121
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Here is my basic north star,

00:52:25.121 --> 00:52:32.480
cooperate where we can, confront
where we must, that's my attitude.

00:52:32.480 --> 00:52:37.690
So, I'm giving Thursday what I think is
a big speech about what we're doing on

00:52:37.690 --> 00:52:41.240
our airport, our public transportation,
our roads, our parks, our schools,

00:52:41.240 --> 00:52:44.630
out community colleges,
and our street lights.

00:52:44.630 --> 00:52:48.310
It's the five year anniversary of
my first speech on infrastructure.

00:52:48.310 --> 00:52:51.830
We're the only city with
a infrastructure bank, etc.

00:52:53.020 --> 00:52:57.340
I'm willing to cooperate, not only
cooperate, do something on infrastructure.

00:52:57.340 --> 00:53:02.860
I'm not sure I understand what the tax
credit idea is, but that would be a place.

00:53:02.860 --> 00:53:08.870
But I won't compromise on
principles about Chicago being

00:53:08.870 --> 00:53:12.710
a welcoming city and a place where
you can dream for your children.

00:53:12.710 --> 00:53:14.950
I will never compromise on that.

00:53:14.950 --> 00:53:19.731
I will never ever allow the city that
gave my grandfather a chance, to turn its

00:53:19.731 --> 00:53:24.513
back on kids whose only reason they're
here is because their parents thought

00:53:24.513 --> 00:53:29.250
that America could offer them something
that their place of origin was not.

00:53:29.250 --> 00:53:35.928
Amy and I, a week ago,
had six dreamers at our house for dinner.

00:53:35.928 --> 00:53:38.890
A kid was from India, there was a kid
from Nigeria, and four kids from Mexico.

00:53:41.760 --> 00:53:44.974
Every one of them are the first in
their family to go to college, or

00:53:44.974 --> 00:53:46.676
thinking about going to college.

00:53:46.676 --> 00:53:48.950
That is power of this country.

00:53:48.950 --> 00:53:52.540
That is the power of this city, and I'll
never compromise on that because it's not

00:53:52.540 --> 00:53:55.050
worth being in public
service giving up on that.

00:53:55.050 --> 00:53:57.890
It's just not.
But

00:53:57.890 --> 00:54:02.550
you want to invest in helping us
build our public transportation?

00:54:02.550 --> 00:54:04.448
Great, I will always take your money.

00:54:04.448 --> 00:54:08.475
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So those type of things,

00:54:08.475 --> 00:54:13.800
that's where I'm willing to do it and
I'll be clear about that, and wanting to.

00:54:14.890 --> 00:54:15.870
I should be quicker on the answer.

00:54:15.870 --> 00:54:16.805
&gt;&gt; Time for one more.

00:54:16.805 --> 00:54:18.140
&gt;&gt; Well, I've got like 20 answers.

00:54:18.140 --> 00:54:19.108
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:54:19.108 --> 00:54:22.050
&gt;&gt; You pick, you got like five hands,
not me, six, right there.

00:54:23.210 --> 00:54:24.650
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Mayor Immanuel.

00:54:24.650 --> 00:54:28.199
Matt, MBA two, from outside of Chicago
&gt;&gt; What does two mean?

00:54:28.199 --> 00:54:28.987
You're second year?

00:54:28.987 --> 00:54:30.769
&gt;&gt; Second year, yeah, sorry.

00:54:30.769 --> 00:54:32.097
[LAUGH] From outside of Chicago.

00:54:32.097 --> 00:54:32.918
&gt;&gt; What's that?

00:54:32.918 --> 00:54:34.704
&gt;&gt; I am from outside Chicago.

00:54:34.704 --> 00:54:39.443
Question is what are you
currently doing to address crime,

00:54:39.443 --> 00:54:43.060
and in particular violent
crime in Chicago?

00:54:43.060 --> 00:54:48.102
I know it is very complicated, but curious
to get some insight beyond the headlines.

00:54:48.102 --> 00:54:53.988
&gt;&gt; Well, first of all, thanks Matt, two.

00:54:53.988 --> 00:54:58.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; A number of things

00:54:58.790 --> 00:55:03.826
that we're doing, and then also
understanding of the issue, okay?

00:55:03.826 --> 00:55:07.702
So, we have 77 neighborhoods in Chicago,

00:55:07.702 --> 00:55:13.210
last year 37 of the 77 experienced
a decline in shootings and

00:55:13.210 --> 00:55:17.904
homicides, and
in January still experiencing that.

00:55:17.904 --> 00:55:24.325
Two neighborhoods or areas, Englewood
which is on the South side in the seventh,

00:55:24.325 --> 00:55:29.071
and the Harrison which is on
the Austin area on the Westside,

00:55:29.071 --> 00:55:33.840
and Garfield,
are driving almost 50% of the challenge.

00:55:35.810 --> 00:55:40.795
So, I laid out at
Malcolm X Community College back in

00:55:40.795 --> 00:55:43.640
September a four point plan.

00:55:43.640 --> 00:55:48.696
One was to hire another 900 plus officers,
and that included lieutenants,

00:55:48.696 --> 00:55:53.677
captains, sergeants, leadership, so
you had a more intimate relationship

00:55:53.677 --> 00:55:57.800
between leadership In rank and file.

00:55:57.800 --> 00:56:01.807
And to, and
more intense training around real life

00:56:01.807 --> 00:56:05.638
experiences they're going to
see in the streets.

00:56:05.638 --> 00:56:09.660
Two, and this gets to a life commitment.

00:56:09.660 --> 00:56:13.010
So, first of all,
I dramatically in my first term and

00:56:13.010 --> 00:56:16.260
second term expanded our after school and
summer jobs.

00:56:16.260 --> 00:56:21.931
So we went from 14,000 kids in
our summer jobs to 31,000 kids.

00:56:21.931 --> 00:56:26.008
I believe in that as a positive effect,
and if you get the job.

00:56:26.008 --> 00:56:29.490
So there's 64,000 kids that apply for
those 31,000.

00:56:29.490 --> 00:56:31.630
You have to pledge to go to college.

00:56:31.630 --> 00:56:34.069
You don't get the job unless you
pledge you're going to go to college.

00:56:36.586 --> 00:56:41.117
We now started for
7,200 young men, 8th, 9th, and

00:56:41.117 --> 00:56:45.830
10th grade,
in our 20 most challenging neighborhoods,

00:56:45.830 --> 00:56:51.650
universal mentoring, four hours a day,
Monday through Friday in school.

00:56:51.650 --> 00:56:54.150
It's a program called BAM, Become a Man.

00:56:55.520 --> 00:57:00.280
I just have Theo Epstein from
the Chicago Cubs come to a circle.

00:57:00.280 --> 00:57:03.060
When President Obama introduce
my brother's keeper,

00:57:04.070 --> 00:57:07.480
the young man Christian who
introduced him was a kid that,

00:57:07.480 --> 00:57:11.520
I have a kid at presidency in 2012
at Highland Park High School.

00:57:11.520 --> 00:57:15.710
And he participated for like an hour and
a half in this BAM circle, it's a circle.

00:57:15.710 --> 00:57:22.666
And the kids really learn how to, They
learn what your father would teach you,

00:57:22.666 --> 00:57:26.891
and they get that kind of guidance
of knowing right from wrong,

00:57:26.891 --> 00:57:30.096
how not to give up on themselves,
how to study.

00:57:30.096 --> 00:57:32.430
There's a whole host of
things that they do.

00:57:32.430 --> 00:57:37.410
The third thing is, unlike New York,
or other cities or California.

00:57:38.480 --> 00:57:40.430
We have a one year minimum for gun crimes.

00:57:40.430 --> 00:57:42.900
In New York,
they have a three year minimum.

00:57:42.900 --> 00:57:47.250
And repeat offenders barely
ever serve a sentence.

00:57:47.250 --> 00:57:48.590
They know it's a joke.

00:57:48.590 --> 00:57:51.940
So I'm for stiffening sentences for
gun crimes.

00:57:51.940 --> 00:57:54.540
Reducing them for minor narcotics.

00:57:54.540 --> 00:57:59.846
But you take somebody's life,
I got no, I'm not, I'm Old Testament.

00:57:59.846 --> 00:58:02.954
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay?

00:58:02.954 --> 00:58:04.485
I am.

00:58:04.485 --> 00:58:05.481
Regardless of what my Rabbi says.

00:58:05.481 --> 00:58:10.268
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My view is we went crazy on narcotics,

00:58:10.268 --> 00:58:13.340
but on gun crimes.

00:58:13.340 --> 00:58:16.510
And then fourth and finally,
invest in the neighborhoods.

00:58:16.510 --> 00:58:18.240
There is despair.

00:58:18.240 --> 00:58:21.765
A lot, it's also true, I don't know,

00:58:21.765 --> 00:58:27.390
San Jose last year experienced
a 60% increase in homicides.

00:58:27.390 --> 00:58:28.270
Right here, next door to you.

00:58:29.630 --> 00:58:34.180
Okay, not the numbers we have,
but they saw a big increase.

00:58:34.180 --> 00:58:38.120
San Diego was up 81% last year.

00:58:39.840 --> 00:58:42.340
So something else is happening and
that's a larger conversation.

00:58:42.340 --> 00:58:47.670
So, more police, more mentoring,
certainty around sentencing for

00:58:47.670 --> 00:58:51.970
gun crimes repeat offenders, and investing
in neighborhoods' economic opportunity.

00:58:51.970 --> 00:58:54.450
So you replace despair with hope.

00:58:54.450 --> 00:58:57.090
Now, I believe that's a big piece.

00:58:57.090 --> 00:58:59.100
So just now let me give you some counter.

00:59:01.080 --> 00:59:05.630
Chicago had the biggest
drop of deep poverty

00:59:05.630 --> 00:59:06.950
of any big city in the United States.

00:59:08.530 --> 00:59:12.138
Deep poverty which is 12,000 or less.

00:59:12.138 --> 00:59:15.660
Now I believe poverty plays a big role, if
you don't think you have hope, you don't

00:59:15.660 --> 00:59:18.662
have a high school degree, you're
going to do something really stupid.

00:59:18.662 --> 00:59:21.850
The reason I'm driving towards high school
degrees, the biggest factor that determine

00:59:21.850 --> 00:59:27.570
whether somebody does something stupid or
not, is their education or lack thereof.

00:59:28.940 --> 00:59:33.120
But it may not be the biggest
factor that we think it is.

00:59:33.120 --> 00:59:34.528
It's a big contributor.

00:59:34.528 --> 00:59:39.952
We have a oversize population and
it's neither in school, in work or

00:59:39.952 --> 00:59:46.910
has any of those two things in their
background and that's a driver, okay.

00:59:46.910 --> 00:59:51.060
I also in the midst of a major
reform of our police department.

00:59:51.060 --> 00:59:55.150
I think we as a country made some mistakes
in the past, and I said this before.

00:59:56.320 --> 00:59:59.705
The reforms should not be done to cops.

00:59:59.705 --> 01:00:02.387
They should be done with cops.

01:00:02.387 --> 01:00:06.570
They are fighting crime and
violent crime in a very serious way.

01:00:06.570 --> 01:00:09.750
You're asking to fight crime and
make serious changes simultaneously.

01:00:11.030 --> 01:00:14.170
They should be part of that
process of making the change.

01:00:14.170 --> 01:00:17.030
Not seen as the change
is being done to them.

01:00:17.030 --> 01:00:19.300
And I think when you look at what's
happened around the country.

01:00:20.860 --> 01:00:24.100
Indianapolis and Memphis have the highest
homicide rate in their history.

01:00:24.100 --> 01:00:25.962
San Jose is up, San Diego is up.

01:00:25.962 --> 01:00:28.561
Austin, Texas, 70%.

01:00:28.561 --> 01:00:30.318
San Antonio, 49%.

01:00:30.318 --> 01:00:32.599
Louisville, 44%.

01:00:32.599 --> 01:00:33.156
But why?

01:00:33.156 --> 01:00:34.170
Why, all of a sudden?

01:00:35.320 --> 01:00:37.810
And I think it was clear
over the last two years,

01:00:39.670 --> 01:00:42.890
things were done as if the police,
they have to make reforms.

01:00:42.890 --> 01:00:45.930
There's no if's, and's,
or but's about it, but

01:00:45.930 --> 01:00:50.680
they have to be brought to the table as
a partner to help think of the solutions.

01:00:50.680 --> 01:00:55.520
Because if you do it to them, they're
going to, so the four points to deal

01:00:55.520 --> 01:01:01.030
with it and is to deal with it
from an economic standpoint,

01:01:01.030 --> 01:01:05.710
a moral standpoint, a criminal justice
standpoint, and making sure your officers

01:01:05.710 --> 01:01:09.840
are doing community policing in the most
intimate way in building that trust.

01:01:09.840 --> 01:01:14.030
And it's, we can do it, but
it's going to take not one.

01:01:14.030 --> 01:01:16.020
And the other thing is,
a lot of people want to look for

01:01:16.020 --> 01:01:19.660
the one thing, you're dealing
with 30 to 40 years of stings.

01:01:21.230 --> 01:01:22.370
Both in the police department,

01:01:22.370 --> 01:01:24.780
in neighborhoods,
in the criminal justice system.

01:01:24.780 --> 01:01:29.120
You're not,
there is on one thing to this challenge.

01:01:30.620 --> 01:01:34.789
And you ask me the toughest things
in the Clinton White House.

01:01:36.280 --> 01:01:38.890
I can handle anything in this job but
one thing.

01:01:40.970 --> 01:01:46.693
So I'd make a purpose to go visit,
usually it's a parent, a mother,

01:01:46.693 --> 01:01:52.040
but not limited, or grandma whose family,
who has lost a child.

01:01:54.980 --> 01:01:55.676
Only if they want it.

01:01:55.676 --> 01:01:57.430
I call them all.

01:01:57.430 --> 01:01:58.500
If you want, I'll come by, but

01:01:58.500 --> 01:02:01.690
if you don't because of
how your feeling I get it.

01:02:05.990 --> 01:02:08.810
And I'll always say,
I'm not here as a mayor.

01:02:10.340 --> 01:02:14.120
I'm here as a father,
because I have three teenagers.

01:02:17.520 --> 01:02:22.450
I don't know how you are doing this, if
you want to just have a hug, just want to

01:02:22.450 --> 01:02:28.890
talk about your son or your daughter,
and that's the hardest part of this job.

01:02:29.980 --> 01:02:33.330
Figuring out a pension,
what's right about school.

01:02:34.870 --> 01:02:37.040
How to recruit people like
you to come to the city.

01:02:38.460 --> 01:02:38.960
It's not hard.

01:02:41.425 --> 01:02:41.980
You go into a home.

01:02:44.120 --> 01:02:47.830
You're a parent, and
maybe some of you are, but

01:02:47.830 --> 01:02:52.490
you'll get there, where the most thing
that you can do which is to provide for

01:02:52.490 --> 01:02:56.730
your child and you just didn't succeed and
all you have is somebody's shoulder.

01:02:57.780 --> 01:02:59.066
That's the hardest part of this job.

01:02:59.066 --> 01:03:05.720
Everything else is politics and
you can handle it.

01:03:05.720 --> 01:03:08.019
But the good news is,
we have a police department and

01:03:08.019 --> 01:03:09.798
a police superintendent leadership and

01:03:09.798 --> 01:03:12.950
we have people in the city that want
to make tomorrow better than today.

01:03:12.950 --> 01:03:15.360
And they are committed to
making the changes necessary.

01:03:15.360 --> 01:03:17.693
My job as mayor is to
help lead that effort and

01:03:17.693 --> 01:03:20.925
bring everybody to the table
to work through the solutions.

01:03:20.925 --> 01:03:27.428
And that's why this is the greatest
job I've ever had in public life.

01:03:27.428 --> 01:03:28.545
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much.

01:03:28.545 --> 01:03:33.453
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

01:03:33.453 --> 01:03:38.680
[MUSIC]

